WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:06.740
My name's Kelly.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.510
I am from OurDeerlyDeparted,

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.610
and I am a taxidermist.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.940
And today, I'm going to show you guys

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.260
how I restore a taxidermy dog.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.450
Apparently, Cutty was a very
famous dog in West Virginia.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.100
She was a dog that lived in a gas station,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.710
so everyone knew her.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.260
She sat in a window for a very long time.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.200
She was sun bleached, so
she lost all of her color.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.820
Her ears were broken,

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.453
and she was missing an eye.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.720
So, the first step is to basically

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.150
go over the animal, see
what's wrong with it,

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.790
and I always check and see if there's any

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.600
remnants of bugs or a
possible infestation.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.180
So, I'm blowing it with a vacuum.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.310
If I don't blow off that dust,

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:56.970
the dirt is going to be
pushed down into the fur

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.300
after I wipe it.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.108
Cutty was very fragile.

00:01:01.108 --> 00:01:02.508
Fur was coming out.

00:01:02.508 --> 00:01:04.740
Her ears were basically falling off.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.340
So certain parts like that,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.290
you have to be super
careful when cleaning it.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.250
I just use rubbing alcohol.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.490
I don't want to use too much of it,

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.540
because it can dry out the animal,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.230
but it's a really good
cleaner for taxidermy.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.830
The black stuff coming
off of her head is paint.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.600
And I just had to work at it,

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.933
be super delicate with it,

00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:25.341
because that wire brush
can tear out the hair

00:01:25.341 --> 00:01:26.600
if you're not careful.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.440
So I was going in circular motions

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.170
and kind of just working the paint off

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.820
and taking a paper towel, wiping it off,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.590
and just being super slow with it

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.457
and really taking my time.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.850
Her ears were both cracked.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.560
This is a really common thing
that can happen with ears.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.880
Her ears had to be restrengthened.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.818
So, what I first did was I actually

00:01:52.818 --> 00:01:54.743
rehydrated them with water.

00:01:54.743 --> 00:01:57.270
I stuffed the ears with some cotton balls,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.430
paper towels,

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.210
just something that
would hold the moisture,

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.700
and wrapped them in Saran wrap.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.620
It's just to kind of keep
everything nice and moist

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.420
so I could work on it.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.960
I let them sit for about a day.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.451
Sometimes with old taxidermy,

00:02:10.451 --> 00:02:12.320
if you rehydrate it too much,

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.530
it could actually fall apart,

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.080
so you really have to watch that.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.310
After the ears were rehydrated,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.050
I filled it first with clay,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.040
and I also filled the ears with Bondo.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.260
That really strengthened
her ears up a lot.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.520
And then after her ears were strengthened,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.186
I went over top of the Bondo

00:02:28.186 --> 00:02:29.702
with some Apoxie Sculpt,

00:02:29.702 --> 00:02:31.310
just to kind of even it out

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.105
and fill in those cracks.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:40.000
Her one eye was really
great, looked awesome,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.580
but her other eye was missing.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.750
When I did start to clean
out what was left of it,

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.110
there was actually some glass.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.753
So what I had to do was actually

00:02:48.753 --> 00:02:51.430
remove the other eye that looked OK,

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.730
because I wasn't able to
match the eyes exactly.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.510
I couldn't find anything to replicate it.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.870
So I didn't want to go in
and put in a different eye

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.220
that was a shade lighter.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.687
Again, I rehydrated it,
removed the other eye,

00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:06.340
and replaced them with
brand-new glass eyes.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:08.550
I actually added some
more clay into the eye

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.430
to give it that extra detail

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.780
and to make the eye sit
in there really nicely.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:16.550
They were actually bear eyes, I believe,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.020
because they don't make dog eyes.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.543
It was about the same
size and color as a dog.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:22.840
When replacing eyes,

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.290
it definitely is tricky,
because it's backwards

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.530
of how you would typically do taxidermy.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:29.500
So, when you're mounting something fresh,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.640
those eyes go on before the skin goes on.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.850
So I'm doing it completely backwards

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.123
and adding the clay in
after the eyes are in there.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:40.960
She was missing, I want to say,

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.270
two or three of her original nails.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.088
Coyote nails were about the closest thing

00:03:46.088 --> 00:03:48.550
I could find to dog nails.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.190
And I just happened to have
a few of them laying around,

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.798
and they worked perfectly.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.230
After all of her parts are repaired,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.630
it's time to airbrush Cutty
back to her original color.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.600
I choose to airbrush taxidermy

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.958
just because it creates a really nice,

00:04:05.958 --> 00:04:07.391
natural look to them.

00:04:07.391 --> 00:04:10.770
You can really control
how much paint is applied,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.770
and you're able to paint really big areas

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.450
with a lot of paint very quickly.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.470
The paints I used are
actually lacquer paints.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.340
They're called LifeTone paints,

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.710
and they are made for taxidermy animals.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:27.770
They're special colors that
are made for painting skin

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.480
and replicating those color tones

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.570
that are found in animals.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:36.380
With Cutty, I was layering
those colors a lot on there.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.930
I started out with a really light yellow

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:41.420
and some of the darker colors on her face,

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.900
and I'd go over with a more orangey color

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:48.120
to make that yellow pop out
from underneath her fur.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.710
What I did for Cutty's muzzle,

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:52.200
she was missing fur.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.690
One way to replace fur is to add flocking.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.810
I went in with a black and brown flocking,

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:00.200
and I mixed it together.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.080
I applied it with Mod Podge.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.040
So just typical craft glue.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.180
Flocking is basically a
crushed velvet material,

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.350
and you're able to
spray that onto the skin

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.580
and recreate a really short fur texture.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.470
And then I painted over
top of that for, again,

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.180
to really tie it all together

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.130
and make the colors look seamless,

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.380
make these pieces last a life.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.190
The tongue was yellow
from it being in the sun.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:31.263
I just painted it with acrylic
paints to restore that color.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.806
Taxidermy pieces usually never need

00:05:37.806 --> 00:05:39.914
to really be maintained or restored

00:05:39.914 --> 00:05:41.800
if they're kept in the right conditions.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.470
I've seen pieces that
are hundreds of years old

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.070
and are in almost perfect condition.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:49.130
Generally, just dusting pieces,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.638
keeping them out of the
sunlight, maintaining,

00:05:51.638 --> 00:05:54.300
and making sure that
there's no infestations

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.583
make these pieces last a lifetime.

